{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (DOCTOR): Seamus, come on in and sit yourself down. I hear you nearly talked yourself out of coming.",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (PATIENT): I'm grand, doctor. Honest. It's a waste of your morning, a small bit of a cough and nothing else.",
  "[SEG0003] person_3 (CAREGIVER): He's not grand. He was puffing coming in from the car park, and the cough has him wrecked every morning.",
  "[SEG0004] person_2 (PATIENT): Yerra, she fusses. Sure everyone hacks a bit when they get up.",
  "[SEG0005] person_1 (DOCTOR): Tell me what that cough is actually like, in your own words.",
  "[SEG0006] person_2 (PATIENT): It's the mornings. A hack, then a bit of stuff comes up, and I'm grand for the rest of the day.",
  "[SEG0007] person_1 (DOCTOR): How long would you say the morning cough has been going on?",
  "[SEG0008] person_2 (PATIENT): Five-ish years. I don't be counting it. It's just part of getting up.",
  "[SEG0009] person_1 (DOCTOR): And this breathlessness, when do you notice it?",
  "[SEG0010] person_2 (PATIENT): Only if I hurry up the hill to the shop. I'm sixty-seven. On the flat I'm flying, not a bother.",
  "[SEG0011] person_3 (CAREGIVER): He stops twice on that path. He's been slower since the summer, whatever he tells you.",
  "[SEG0012] person_1 (DOCTOR): Has the shortness of breath been there the whole time, or is it newer?",
  "[SEG0013] person_2 (PATIENT): The last few months, I'd say. Only if I hurry on that hill, and on the flat I'm grand. It's not that bad.",
  "[SEG0014] person_1 (DOCTOR): Any pain in the chest when it comes on?",
  "[SEG0015] person_2 (PATIENT): No. No pain. Nothing like that.",
  "[SEG0016] person_1 (DOCTOR): Have you coughed up any blood?",
  "[SEG0017] person_2 (PATIENT): God, no. Just the usual bit of muck in the morning.",
  "[SEG0018] person_1 (DOCTOR): What colour is that phlegm these days?",
  "[SEG0019] person_2 (PATIENT): White, a bit grey. It went green when I had the infection, but that's well gone.",
  "[SEG0020] person_3 (CAREGIVER): That infection was about three weeks back. He was floored for a few days.",
  "[SEG0021] person_2 (PATIENT): A couple of days in the bed, not floored. Out-of-hours gave me tablets and I finished every one of them.",
  "[SEG0022] person_1 (DOCTOR): Do you remember the name, and how you took them?",
  "[SEG0023] person_2 (PATIENT): Amoxicillin, for that chest infection. Five hundred milligrams, three times a day, for a week. Done and dusted.",
  "[SEG0024] person_1 (DOCTOR): Any fever now, or sweats in the night?",
  "[SEG0025] person_2 (PATIENT): Not now. I had a temperature with the infection, that's settled, and no sweats.",
  "[SEG0026] person_1 (DOCTOR): Are you using any inhalers at the moment?",
  "[SEG0027] person_2 (PATIENT): The blue one. Ventolin. Two puffs when I think of it. Some days twice, some days I don't bother at all.",
  "[SEG0028] person_3 (CAREGIVER): The box from last month is still nearly full. It lives in the kitchen drawer.",
  "[SEG0029] person_1 (DOCTOR): So it isn't really being taken the way it was meant. What strength is on the canister?",
  "[SEG0030] person_2 (PATIENT): A hundred micrograms, I think. I never look at it properly.",
  "[SEG0031] person_1 (DOCTOR): Any other medicines at all?",
  "[SEG0032] person_2 (PATIENT): Nothing regular. I don't take tablets if I can help it.",
  "[SEG0033] person_1 (DOCTOR): Any allergies to medicines, or foods, or anything else?",
  "[SEG0034] person_2 (PATIENT): Not a one. Never had an allergy in my life.",
  "[SEG0035] person_1 (DOCTOR): Do you smoke, Seamus?",
  "[SEG0036] person_2 (PATIENT): About ten a day. Sure that's hardly smoking at all.",
  "[SEG0037] person_3 (CAREGIVER): It's not ten. I have a packet dropped from the shop every day, and it's gone by night.",
  "[SEG0038] person_2 (PATIENT): Well, actually, maybe twenty. Some days. I'm after cutting down, nearly.",
  "[SEG0039] person_1 (DOCTOR): And how long have you been smoking?",
  "[SEG0040] person_2 (PATIENT): Forty-odd years. Since I was on the buildings. Everyone had a cigarette then.",
  "[SEG0041] person_1 (DOCTOR): Are you retired off the buildings now, and who is at home with you?",
  "[SEG0042] person_2 (PATIENT): Retired these ten years. I live on my own. Herself only comes in the mornings.",
  "[SEG0043] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Five mornings a week. Evenings he does himself, tea and the telly.",
  "[SEG0044] person_1 (DOCTOR): Any lung trouble, or heart trouble, in the family?",
  "[SEG0045] person_2 (PATIENT): My father had emphysema near the end. Smoked worse than me, mind you.",
  "[SEG0046] person_1 (DOCTOR): Grand, I'll have a listen to the chest, and then we'll look at the blowing test from this morning.",
  "[SEG0047] person_1 (DOCTOR): Chest is moving evenly. There are a few crackles at the bases, no wheeze today. Heart sounds are fine, and your ankles aren't swollen.",
  "[SEG0048] person_1 (DOCTOR): Blood pressure is one-thirty over eighty-four. Pulse is seventy-eight. Your oxygen is ninety-four percent on air, and the temperature was thirty-six point six.",
  "[SEG0049] person_1 (DOCTOR): The spirometry, I'll read it out. FEV1 is one point eight litres, fifty-eight percent of predicted. FVC is three point two litres, and the ratio is point five six.",
  "[SEG0050] person_1 (DOCTOR): I'll be straight with you. My first thought was to watch and wait, no new inhaler, and see you in three months.",
  "[SEG0051] person_1 (DOCTOR): I'm changing that, though. With an FEV1 like that I don't want to leave it sitting.",
  "[SEG0052] person_3 (CAREGIVER): What are you thinking, then, doctor?",
  "[SEG0053] person_1 (DOCTOR): I'd say this is likely COPD from the years of smoking. Provisional, mind you, until the x-ray and how you get on. I'll start you on Spiriva, eighteen micrograms, inhaled once a day, every day.",
  "[SEG0054] person_1 (DOCTOR): We'll book a chest x-ray this week. And I'll refer you on to the stop-smoking service. They'll ring you.",
  "[SEG0055] person_1 (DOCTOR): Come back to me in four weeks. Keep using the blue Ventolin, two puffs when you're breathless, and the Spiriva has to be every morning, not when you feel like it.",
  "[SEG0056] person_2 (PATIENT): It's a lot of fuss for a smoker's cough. I'll try the new one, I suppose.",
  "[SEG0057] person_1 (DOCTOR): If the breathlessness gets worse, or you cough up blood, or a fever comes back, don't wait the four weeks. Come straight back, or go to out-of-hours.",
  "[SEG0058] person_3 (CAREGIVER): I'll leave the two inhalers beside the kettle, so he sees them with the tea. He'll still say he's grand.",
  "[SEG0059] person_2 (PATIENT): I am grand. We'll see."
 ],
